          {
            "id": 2918709,
            "level": 1,
            "value": "Four. You will hear part of a lecture on the early history of keeping clean. First, you have some time to look at questions 31 to 40. Now listen carefully and answer questions 31 to 40.",
            "children": [
              {
                "id": 2918710,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Four.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 0.71999997,
                  "from": 0.0,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 0"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2918712,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "You will hear part of a lecture on the early history of keeping clean.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 6.48,
                  "from": 1.68,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 0"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2918727,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "First, you have some time to look at questions 31 to 40.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 12.48,
                  "from": 7.7599998,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 0"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2918737,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Now listen carefully and answer questions 31 to 40.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 67.2,
                  "from": 62.72,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 0"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": 2918747,
            "level": 1,
            "value": "Nowadays, we use different products for personal cleanliness, laundry, dishwashing, and household cleaning, but this is very much a twentieth century development. The origins of cleanliness date back to prehistoric times. Since water is essential for life, the earliest people lived near water and knew something about its cleansing properties, at least that it rinsed mud off their hands. During the excavation of ancient Babylon, evidence was found that soap making was known as early as February. Archaeologists discovered cylinders made of clay with inscriptions on them saying that fats were boiled with ashes.",
            "children": [
              {
                "id": 2918748,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Nowadays, we use different products for personal cleanliness, laundry, dishwashing, and household cleaning, but this is very much a twentieth century development.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 81.815,
                  "from": 70.535,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2918771,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "The origins of cleanliness date back to prehistoric times.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 87.14,
                  "from": 82.82,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2918781,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Since water is essential for life, the earliest people lived near water and knew something about its cleansing properties, at least that it rinsed mud off their hands.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 99.965004,
                  "from": 87.78,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2918810,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "During the excavation of ancient Babylon, evidence was found that soap making was known as early as February.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 110.605,
                  "from": 100.76501,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2918829,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Archaeologists discovered cylinders made of clay with inscriptions on them saying that fats were boiled with ashes.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 120.25,
                  "from": 111.770004,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": 2918847,
            "level": 1,
            "value": "This is a method of making soap, though there's no reference to the purpose of this material. The early Greeks bathed for aesthetic reasons and apparently didn't use soap. Instead, they cleaned their bodies with blocks of sand, pumice, and ashes, then anointed themselves with oil and scraped off the oil and dirt with a metal instrument known as a strigil. They also used oil mixed with ashes. Clothes were washed without soap in streams.",
            "children": [
              {
                "id": 2918848,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "This is a method of making soap, though there's no reference to the purpose of this material.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 125.61,
                  "from": 120.65,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2918866,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "The early Greeks bathed for aesthetic reasons and apparently didn't use soap.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 132.625,
                  "from": 127.185,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2918879,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Instead, they cleaned their bodies with blocks of sand, pumice, and ashes, then anointed themselves with oil and scraped off the oil and dirt with a metal instrument known as a strigil.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 147.6,
                  "from": 133.025,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2918912,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "They also used oil mixed with ashes.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 150.64,
                  "from": 148.08,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2918920,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Clothes were washed without soap in streams.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 155.20502,
                  "from": 151.445,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": 2918928,
            "level": 1,
            "value": "The ancient Germans and Gauls are also credited with discovering how to make a substance called soap made of melted animal fat and ashes. They used this mixture to tint their hair red. Soap got its name, according to an ancient Roman legend, from Mount Sappo, where animals were sacrificed, leaving deposits of animal fat. Rain washed these deposits along with wood ashes down into the clay soil along the River Tiber. Women found that this mixture greatly reduced the effort required to wash their clothes.",
            "children": [
              {
                "id": 2918929,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "The ancient Germans and Gauls are also credited with discovering how to make a substance called soap made of melted animal fat and ashes.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 166.725,
                  "from": 156.405,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2918954,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "They used this mixture to tint their hair red.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 171.20001,
                  "from": 167.36,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2918964,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Soap got its name, according to an ancient Roman legend, from Mount Sappo, where animals were sacrificed, leaving deposits of animal fat.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 182.48,
                  "from": 172.32,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2918987,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Rain washed these deposits along with wood ashes down into the clay soil along the River Tiber.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 190.565,
                  "from": 183.52501,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2919005,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Women found that this mixture greatly reduced the effort required to wash their clothes.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 196.485,
                  "from": 190.88501,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": 2919020,
            "level": 1,
            "value": "As Roman civilization advanced, so did bathing. The first of the famous Roman baths supplied with water from their aqueducts was built around March. The baths were luxurious, and bathing became very popular. And by the second century AD, the Greek physician Galen recommended soap for both medicinal and cleansing purposes. After the fall of Rome in April and the resulting decline in bathing habits, much of Europe felt the impact of filth on public health.",
            "children": [
              {
                "id": 2919021,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "As Roman civilization advanced, so did bathing.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 201.98001,
                  "from": 197.9,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2919029,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "The first of the famous Roman baths supplied with water from their aqueducts was built around March.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 210.86,
                  "from": 202.62,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2919047,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "The baths were luxurious, and bathing became very popular.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 216.575,
                  "from": 211.775,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2919057,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "And by the second century AD, the Greek physician Galen recommended soap for both medicinal and cleansing purposes.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 226.495,
                  "from": 217.055,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2919076,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "After the fall of Rome in April and the resulting decline in bathing habits, much of Europe felt the impact of filth on public health.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 243.285,
                  "from": 231.86,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": 2919102,
            "level": 1,
            "value": "This lack of personal cleanliness and related unsanitary living conditions were major factors in the outbreaks of disease in the Middle Ages and especially the Black Death of the fourteenth century. Nevertheless, soap making became an established craft in Europe, and associations of soap makers guarded their trade secrets closely. Vegetable and animal oils were used with ashes of plants along with perfume, apparently for the first time. Gradually, more varieties of soap became available for shaving and shampooing as well as bathing and laundering. A major step toward large scale commercial soap making occurred in 1791 when a French chemist Nicolas LeBlanc patented a process for turning salt into soda ash or sodium carbonate.",
            "children": [
              {
                "id": 2919103,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "This lack of personal cleanliness and related unsanitary living conditions were major factors in the outbreaks of disease in the Middle Ages and especially the Black Death of the fourteenth century.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 257.125,
                  "from": 244.005,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2919135,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Nevertheless, soap making became an established craft in Europe, and associations of soap makers guarded their trade secrets closely.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 267.44,
                  "from": 258.24002,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2919154,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Vegetable and animal oils were used with ashes of plants along with perfume, apparently for the first time.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 275.835,
                  "from": 268.16,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2919173,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Gradually, more varieties of soap became available for shaving and shampooing as well as bathing and laundering.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 284.07498,
                  "from": 276.715,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2919191,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "A major step toward large scale commercial soap making occurred in 1791 when a French chemist Nicolas LeBlanc patented a process for turning salt into soda ash or sodium carbonate.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 300.28,
                  "from": 285.31998,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": 2919222,
            "level": 1,
            "value": "Soda ash is the alkali obtained from ashes that combines with fat to form soap. The LeBlanc process yielded quantities of good quality inexpensive soda ash. Modern soap making was born some twenty years later in the early nineteenth century with the discovery by Michel Eugene Chevreul, another French chemist of the chemical nature and relationship of fats, glycerine, and fatty acids. His studies established the basis for both fat and soap chemistry, and soap making became a science. Further developments during the nineteenth century made it easier and cheaper to manufacture soap.",
            "children": [
              {
                "id": 2919223,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Soda ash is the alkali obtained from ashes that combines with fat to form soap.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 308.455,
                  "from": 301.495,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2919239,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "The LeBlanc process yielded quantities of good quality inexpensive soda ash.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 314.455,
                  "from": 308.935,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2919251,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Modern soap making was born some twenty years later in the early nineteenth century with the discovery by Michel Eugene Chevreul, another French chemist of the chemical nature and relationship of fats, glycerine, and fatty acids.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 331.95502,
                  "from": 315.68,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2919288,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "His studies established the basis for both fat and soap chemistry, and soap making became a science.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 339.71503,
                  "from": 332.595,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2919306,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Further developments during the nineteenth century made it easier and cheaper to manufacture soap.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 346.71002,
                  "from": 340.435,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": 2919321,
            "level": 1,
            "value": "Until the nineteenth century, soap was regarded as a luxury item and was heavily taxed in several countries. As it became more readily available, it became an everyday necessity, a development that was reinforced when the high tax was removed. Soap was then something ordinary people could afford, and cleanliness standards improved. With this widespread use came the development of milder soaps for bathing and soaps for use in the washing machines that were available to consumers by the turn of the twentieth century.",
            "children": [
              {
                "id": 2919322,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Until the nineteenth century, soap was regarded as a luxury item and was heavily taxed in several countries.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 354.87003,
                  "from": 347.43,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2919341,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "As it became more readily available, it became an everyday necessity, a development that was reinforced when the high tax was removed.",
                "meta": {
